 ## Overview
-Screen-space post-processing is a full-screen rendering pass that applies aspect-ratio preserving UV covering, luminance-based bright passes, color grading, and spatial effects (such as bloom, blur, and vignetting) to a scene or accumulation texture to produce a display-ready image [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from ray-casting compositing methods by operating in screen space on the rendered buffers rather than during volumetric ray traversal, allowing for complex aesthetic effects like underwater refraction and tone mapping without modifying the volume rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial].
